===============
| Java Arrays |
===============
👉An array is a data structure that stores multiple values of the same data type in a single variable.

int[] marks = {90, 85, 78, 92, 88};

  👉 Stores multiple values.
  👉 Stores same data type only.
  👉 Index starts from 0.
  👉 Fixed size.
  👉 Stored in contiguous memory (conceptually for Java arrays).
  👉 Faster access using index.

datatype[] arrayName = new datatype[size];

int[] numbers = new int[5];                   int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};    // Array Initialization
int numbers[] = new int[5];

numbers → array name
int → data type
5 → size
-----------------------------------
int[] numbers = new int[5];   // Assigning Values
numbers[0] = 10;
numbers[1] = 20;
numbers[2] = 30;
numbers[3] = 40;
numbers[4] = 50;
--------------------------------------------

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = new int[5];
        numbers[0] = 10;
        numbers[1] = 20;
        numbers[2] = 30;
        numbers[3] = 40;
        numbers[4] = 50;
        System.out.println(numbers[0]);
        System.out.println(numbers[1]);
        System.out.println(numbers[2]);
        System.out.println(numbers[3]);
        System.out.println(numbers[4]);
    }
}

  👉 Array stores multiple values of the same data type.
  👉 Index always starts from 0.
  👉 Array size is fixed after creation.
  👉 Elements are accessed using their index.
  👉 Arrays can be created with new or initialized directly using {}.

